**Operator overloading** means defining a special method so an operator works on your own objects. What does this print?

```python
class Vector:
    def __init__(self, x, y):
        self.x = x
        self.y = y

    def __add__(self, other):
        return Vector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)

w = Vector(1, 2) + Vector(10, 20)
print(w.x, w.y)
```

What does this print?

```python
class Sensor:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def describe(self):
        return "sensor " + self.name

class Thermometer(Sensor):
    pass

t = Thermometer("T1")
print(t.describe())
```
